   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to establish a heightened review of new permits for the operation of\ncertain electric generation facilities to ensure consistency with state public\ninterest standards; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon the\nexpiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8809",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8809-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Energy Conservation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8809\n\nSPONSOR: METZGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to establish a heightened review of new permits for the operation\nof certain electric generation facilities to ensure consistency with\nstate public interest standards; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon the expiration thereof\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 is the legislative intent.\n\nSection 2 amends unconsolidated law to require the department of envi-\nronmental conservation (DEC) to deny a new permit for the operation of\nan electric generating station powered by natural gas or any carbon fuel\nunless the facility demonstrates that the station can operate in the\npublic interest, which includes operating within the state's climate\ngoals and avoiding impacts that negatively affect public health or safe-\nty, agriculture, viewshed, tourism and recreational opportunities, or\ncommunity character. The DEC shall make a written determination on\nwhether the facility is in the public interest only after a public hear-\ning and comment period.\n\nSection 3 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill will require a new level of review for power plants that burn\nfossil fuels within the Town of Wawayanda, in Orange County.  This new\nlevel of review is essential to ensure conformity with the goals of the\nClimate Leadership and Community Protection Act (CLCPA), signed into law\nin July 2019, as well as to protect the health and well-being of the\npeople of Wawayanda and surrounding communities and the economic vitali-\nty of the region. In recognition of the dangerous impacts of the burning\nof fossil fuels on the global climate, the CLCPA requires the elimi-\nnation of fossil fuel-based generation entirely by 2040, with an interim\ngoal of meeting 70 percent of the state's electricity needs from renewa-\nble resources by 2030. Within this context, new permits for fossil fuel-\nbased generation enable continued reliance on fuels that are considered\ncontrary to state goals instead of encouraging renewable alternatives.\nFurthermore, the granting of permits for such facilities should meet a\npublic interest standard, and should not be permitted if such facilities\ncould adversely affect public health, community character, or the\neconomic vitality of surrounding communities.\n\nPassage of this legislation will best ensure that the public interest\nwill be protected and that the state's climate goals will be achieved.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and expire and be deemed repealed\non and after December 31, 2020.\n",
